CAT = "nzlcqObject"; SEG = "OBJ"
Q = [
("The object of the Sale and Supply of Alcohol Act 2012 is that alcohol is sold, supplied and consumed safely and responsibly, and that:",
 "Alcohol-related harm is minimised", ["Alcohol prices stay affordable", "Licensed venues stay profitable", "Drinking moves into private homes"],
 "Section 4 sets a two-part object: safe and responsible sale, supply and consumption, and minimising the harm caused by excessive or inappropriate drinking. Every licensing decision is measured against it."),
("Under the Act, harm caused by excessive or inappropriate drinking includes:",
 "Crime, injury and disorder", ["Only drink-driving crashes", "Only long-term liver disease", "Only harm to the drinker"],
 "The Act's definition is wide: crime, damage, death, disease, disorderly behaviour, illness and injury, and harm to society generally caused by those things."),
("Which body decides most licence and manager's certificate applications in a district?",
 "The district licensing committee", ["The local police district commander", "The Medical Officer of Health", "ARLA, the national authority"],
 "Each territorial authority has a district licensing committee (DLC) that hears and decides applications in its district. ARLA is the national body above it."),
("What is ARLA's main role under the Act?",
 "Hearing appeals and enforcement cases", ["Inspecting every bar each weekend night", "Running the alcohol excise tax", "Training and certifying duty managers"],
 "The Alcohol Regulatory and Licensing Authority hears appeals from DLC decisions and applications by the police or inspectors to suspend or cancel licences and certificates."),
("Which three agencies report to the DLC on licence applications?",
 "Police, inspector, Medical Officer of Health", ["Police, WorkSafe and the Customs Service", "Inspector, Fire and Emergency, Inland Revenue", "Council, ARLA and Health New Zealand"],
 "The police, the council's licensing inspector and the Medical Officer of Health each inquire into an application and may oppose it. They are often called the reporting agencies."),
("Who appoints a district's alcohol licensing inspectors?",
 "The territorial authority", ["The New Zealand Police", "The licensing authority (ARLA)", "The Ministry of Justice"],
 "Licensing inspectors are appointed by the territorial authority (the council). They inquire into applications, monitor licensed premises and can apply to ARLA for enforcement action."),
("A Medical Officer of Health looks at a licence application mainly from the point of view of:",
 "Public health and alcohol harm", ["Food hygiene and kitchen grades", "Building and fire compliance", "Employment law for bar staff"],
 "The Medical Officer of Health inquires into applications with a focus on health harm from alcohol in the community and may oppose an application on those grounds."),
("What is a local alcohol policy (LAP)?",
 "A council's own alcohol rules", ["A venue's house policy on ID", "A police code for bar checks", "A national list of approved drinks"],
 "A territorial authority may adopt a LAP, after consulting its community, setting local rules on matters such as trading hours and where licensed premises may be."),
("Which of these can a local alcohol policy set?",
 "Maximum trading hours", ["The price of a standard drink", "Drink-driving limits", "The legal purchase age"],
 "A LAP can set maximum trading hours, limit the location or density of licensed premises, set one-way door restrictions and list discretionary conditions. Purchase age and drink-driving limits are national law."),
("A LAP limits new bottle stores near schools. Which kind of rule is this?",
 "A location restriction", ["A trading hours rule", "A one-way door rule", "A host responsibility rule"],
 "LAPs may limit the location of licensed premises, including by proximity to schools, churches or other facilities, to reduce harm in the community."),
("A one-way door restriction means that after a set time:",
 "Patrons may leave but not enter", ["Only one door may be unlocked", "Staff may not leave early", "Only members may buy drinks"],
 "Under a one-way door restriction, from a stated hour no new patrons may be admitted, though people already inside may stay until closing and may leave."),
("When a council has a LAP in force, a DLC deciding an application must:",
 "Have regard to the LAP", ["Ignore it until it is reviewed", "Refer the case to ARLA first", "Apply only the national defaults"],
 "The criteria a DLC weighs include any relevant LAP. A licence inconsistent with the LAP may be refused or granted with conditions."),
("When deciding an application, the DLC must consider the suitability of:",
 "The applicant", ["The applicant's bank", "Nearby competing venues", "The building's landlord"],
 "Suitability of the applicant is a core criterion: character, history of compliance, convictions and understanding of the Act all count."),
("The DLC must consider whether a new licence would reduce the 'amenity and good order' of the locality. This refers to:",
 "How pleasant and orderly the area is", ["How many shops sell food there", "How profitable the venue will be each year", "How far the venue is from police"],
 "Amenity and good order covers the pleasantness and peace of the neighbourhood: noise, litter, vandalism, nuisance and disorder linked to licensed premises."),
("Which factor does the DLC weigh about how the premises will be run?",
 "Its systems, staff and training", ["The brand of beer on tap", "The owner's other businesses", "The number of television screens"],
 "The criteria include whether the applicant has appropriate systems, staff and training to comply with the law."),
("An applicant's design plan shows a hidden back room where staff cannot see patrons. Why might the DLC care?",
 "It makes monitoring harder", ["It breaches food hygiene law", "Back rooms are never allowed", "It lowers the venue's rent"],
 "The design and layout of premises is a licensing criterion because poor sightlines make it harder to spot intoxication, minors and trouble."),
("A neighbour objects to a new tavern licence on the grounds of late-night noise. Whose decision is it?",
 "The district licensing committee", ["The neighbour's own lawyer", "The Medical Officer of Health", "The tavern's licensee and its owner"],
 "Objections are heard by the DLC, which weighs them alongside the reporting agencies' reports and the criteria in the Act."),
("A party unhappy with a DLC's decision on a licence usually appeals to:",
 "ARLA", ["The local council's mayor", "The licensing inspector", "The Medical Officer of Health"],
 "Appeals from DLC decisions go to the Alcohol Regulatory and Licensing Authority, with further appeal on a point of law to the High Court."),
("Who may apply to ARLA to suspend a manager's certificate?",
 "The police or a licensing inspector", ["Any patron of the premises", "The holder's licensee only", "Only the local Medical Officer of Health"],
 "The police and licensing inspectors can apply to ARLA to vary, suspend or cancel a licence or a manager's certificate, for example after sales to minors."),
("Which law did the Sale and Supply of Alcohol Act 2012 replace?",
 "The Sale of Liquor Act 1989", ["The Licensing Act 1908", "The Liquor Control Act 1962", "The Alcohol Reform Act 2010"],
 "The 2012 Act replaced the Sale of Liquor Act 1989, with stronger rules on intoxication, supply to minors and local control through LAPs."),
("Responsible host practices help meet the Act's object mainly because they:",
 "Reduce alcohol-related harm", ["Raise the average bar spend", "Shorten trading hours by law", "Remove the need for a licence"],
 "Host responsibility aims to prevent intoxication and the harm that follows, which is the second limb of the Act's object."),
("Health New Zealand's alcohol resources site (alcohol.org.nz) mainly provides:",
 "Guidance for licensees and staff", ["Licence application forms and fees", "Court records of offences", "Price lists for alcohol"],
 "The alcohol team now in Health New Zealand (formerly the Health Promotion Agency) publishes host responsibility guides, signs and the intoxication assessment tool."),
("A DLC may refuse a licence if the applicant has shown little understanding of the Act. Which criterion is this?",
 "Suitability of the applicant", ["Amenity and good order", "Days and hours of trade", "Design and layout"],
 "Knowledge of the Act and the licence conditions goes to suitability; inspectors often test it at interview."),
("Why does the Act let communities shape local alcohol rules through a LAP?",
 "Harm differs from place to place", ["National rules no longer apply", "Councils collect alcohol tax", "Police cannot enforce the Act"],
 "Alcohol harm and community views vary by district, so the Act lets each council set local rules within the national framework."),
]
